.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 31, 2026
.Dt JAILER 1
.Os FreeBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm jailer
.Nd build, test, and publish FreeBSD jail OCI images for Legion
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -state-dir Ar dir
.Op Fl -zfs-root Ar dataset | Fl -no-zfs
.Op Fl -plain-http Ar host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
.Ar command
.Op Ar args ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
scaffolds
.Pa Containerfile
definitions, builds FreeBSD OCI container images, tests them locally in
FreeBSD jails matching Legion's runtime, exports offline OCI archives, and
pushes images to OCI distribution registries.
.Pp
Image layers are stored as ZFS datasets
.Ar dataset Ns Pa /images/ Ns Ar chain Ns Pa @layer
and containers as clones under
.Ar dataset Ns Pa /jails .
When run as root on a host with a
.Ql zroot
pool,
.Ar dataset
defaults to
.Ql zroot/jailer ;
otherwise plain directories under the state directory are used.
.Pp
The global options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -state-dir Ar dir
Directory holding images and container state.
Defaults to
.Pa /var/db/jailer
for root and
.Pa ~/.local/share/jailer
otherwise.
.It Fl -zfs-root Ar dataset
ZFS dataset for image layers and container clones.
It is created if missing.
.It Fl -no-zfs
Use plain directory storage even when ZFS is available.
Image files keep their flags, so the state directory holds files marked
.Cm schg ,
as FreeBSD's base system does; to remove it by hand, run
.Ql chflags -R noschg
on it first.
.It Fl -plain-http Ar host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
Talk to this registry over HTTP instead of HTTPS.
May be given more than once.
.El
.Pp
Commands that take
.Fl -creds Ar user Ns Op : Ns Ar password
or
.Fl -authfile Ar path
otherwise read credentials from the Docker or Podman
.Pa auth.json
files, for the registry of each image they pull or push.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Nm init
.Op Fl f
.Op Fl n Ar name
.Op Fl r Ar release
.Op Fl T Ar template
.Op Ar dir
.Xc
Scaffold a
.Pa Containerfile
and
.Pa .containerignore
in
.Ar dir
.Pq default Pa \&. .
.Ar template
is one of
.Cm rust , go , node , python , c , pkg
or
.Cm runtime ,
and is detected from the project files when omitted.
.Ar release
is the FreeBSD base image tag, by default the host's release.
A project with a
.Pa .dockerignore
gets no
.Pa .containerignore ,
which would take the place of its rules.
.Fl f
overwrites an existing
.Pa Containerfile .
.It Xo
.Nm build
.Op Fl -no-cache
.Op Fl -squash
.Op Fl -strict
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl t Ar name Ns Op : Ns Ar tag
.Op Fl -annotation Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl -authfile Ar path
.Op Fl -backend Cm native | buildah
.Op Fl -build-arg Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl -cache-from Ar ref
.Op Fl -cache-max Ar size
.Op Fl -cache-to Ar ref
.Op Fl -compression Cm gzip | zstd
.Op Fl -cpus Ar n
.Op Fl -cpuset-cpus Ar list
.Op Fl -creds Ar user Ns Op : Ns Ar password
.Op Fl -label Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl m Ar size
.Op Fl -param Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl -pids-limit Ar n
.Op Fl -secret Cm id Ns = Ns Ar id , Ns Cm src Ns = Ns Ar file
.Op Fl -ssh Ar id Ns Op = Ns Ar socket
.Op Fl -sysv Cm msg , Ns Cm sem , Ns Cm shm
.Op Fl -target Ar stage
.Op Fl -timestamp Ar seconds
.Op Ar context
.Xc
Build an image from
.Ar file
.Pq default Ar context Ns Pa /Containerfile
and check it for compatibility with Legion.
The image is tagged
.Ar name : Ns Ar tag ,
by default
.Ql localhost/ Ns Ar dir Ns :latest .
.Cm RUN
steps execute in a jail and need root.
A
.Cm RUN
step whose command, environment, user, working directory and preceding
layers match an earlier build reuses that build's layer instead of
running;
.Fl -no-cache
runs every step.
Cached layers are kept, also for a multi-stage build's intermediate
stages, until
.Nm prune Fl -cache ,
or until those no image uses outgrow
.Ar size
(compressed; default
.Ql 10g ,
or
.Cm none
for no limit), when a build drops the least recently used.
.Pp
Only the stage asked for, by
.Fl -target
or as the last, and the stages it needs are built: those it is built on or
copies from, through
.Cm FROM ,
.Cm COPY Fl -from ,
a bind mount's
.Cm from ,
or an
.Cm ONBUILD
trigger.
A source given by a variable keeps every stage before it.
.Pp
A
.Cm RUN
step may take, as in Docker,
.Fl -network Ns = Ns Cm none
to run without network, and mounts that are never part of a layer:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -mount Ns = Ns Cm type=cache , Ns Cm target Ns = Ns Ar path
A directory kept between builds, for package and compiler caches.
.Cm id
names it (default
.Ar path ) ;
.Cm mode , uid , gid
set it up when first made, and
.Cm ro
mounts it read-only; changing any of them runs the step again.
.Cm sharing
says how builds at the same time use it:
.Cm shared
(the default) all at once,
.Cm locked
one at a time, and
.Cm private
one, the others each getting an empty cache of their own.
.It Fl -mount Ns = Ns Cm type=bind , Ns Cm target Ns = Ns Ar path
The build context, less what
.Pa .containerignore
leaves out, or with
.Cm from Ns = Ns Ar stage
an earlier stage's files, or an image's;
.Cm source
picks a path in it (default the whole).
Read-only, unless
.Cm rw ,
whose writes are discarded.
The step runs again when the mounted files change.
.It Fl -mount Ns = Ns Cm type=secret , Ns Cm id Ns = Ns Ar id
The secret given by
.Fl -secret Cm id Ns = Ns Ar id , Ns Cm src Ns = Ns Ar file
(or
.Cm env Ns = Ns Ar var ) ,
as a file at
.Cm target
(default
.Pa /run/secrets/ Ns Ar id ) ,
mode 0400 unless
.Cm mode
says otherwise.
A step runs again when its secret's
.Cm mode , uid
or
.Cm gid
changes, but not when its value does.
A missing secret is skipped, or fails the build with
.Cm required .
.It Fl -mount Ns = Ns Cm type=ssh
The SSH agent given by
.Fl -ssh Ar id Ns Op = Ns Ar socket
.Po
.Cm id
defaults to
.Cm default ,
and
.Ar socket
to
.Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK ,
which
.Xr sudo 8
does not pass on
.Pc ,
as a socket at
.Cm target
(default
.Pa /run/buildkit/ssh_agent.0 ) ,
named by
.Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK
in the step.
The socket is there for the step only, and in no layer; the rest of
its directory stays as the image has it.
A target already in the image fails the build.
The socket has mode 0600 and belongs to root unless
.Cm mode , uid
and
.Cm gid
say otherwise.
A missing agent is skipped, or fails the build with
.Cm required .
.It Fl -mount Ns = Ns Cm type=tmpfs , Ns Cm target Ns = Ns Ar path
An empty memory file system, of at most
.Cm size
bytes
.Pq e.g. Ql 64m .
.El
.Pp
Also as in Docker:
.Bl -bullet -width Ds
.It
Here-documents.
.Ql RUN <<EOF
runs the lines up to
.Ql EOF
as a script, with the interpreter a
.Ql #!
line names;
.Ql RUN cmd <<EOF
gives them to the shell, and
.Ql COPY <<name dest
writes them to a file
.Ar name ,
with variables expanded unless the word is quoted, as in
.Ql <<'name' .
There, as in BuildKit, a backslash takes the character after it as it
is:
.Ql \e$A
is a
.Ql $
and
.Ql \e\e
a backslash.
.It
.Cm COPY Fl -from Ns = Ns Ar image ,
naming an image rather than a stage; it is pulled if missing.
.Fl -from
may use build arguments, as may
.Cm RUN Fl -mount Ns = Ns Cm type=bind,from= .
A copy keeps hard links among the files it copies, and a copy from a
stage or an image keeps their owners and file flags too, unless
.Fl -chown
gives an owner; from the context, files are root's.
Symbolic links on the way to a source or destination are followed as
in a jail on that root: an absolute link starts from the image's (or
the context's) root, and
.Ql ..
stops there, so a copy never reads or writes outside it.
.It
.Cm COPY Fl -exclude Ns = Ns Ar pattern
(matched below each source, as
.Pa .containerignore
patterns are below the context: one without
.Ql /
names a file at any depth, a leading
.Ql /
anchors it, and only
.Ql **
crosses directories),
.Fl -parents ,
which keeps each source's path below the destination
(or the part after
.Ql /./ ) ,
and
.Fl -link ,
accepted with no effect.
Wildcards may appear in any part of a source path.
.It
.Cm ADD Ar url ,
downloaded with mode 0600 and not unpacked, and checked with
.Fl -checksum Ns = Ns Cm sha256: Ns Ar hex .
A local file that is a tar archive, as it is or compressed with gzip,
bzip2, xz or zstd, is unpacked, whatever its name (as BuildKit finds
one: by its contents); another file is copied, whatever its name;
.Fl -chown
and
.Fl -chmod
apply to what it holds.
.It
.Cm ONBUILD ,
whose instructions are kept in the image and run by builds that use it
as a base.
.It
The
.Ql # escape=
parser directive;
.Ql # syntax=
is ignored with a warning.
.It
Variables:
.Ql $VAR ,
.Ql ${VAR} ,
.Ql ${VAR:-word} ,
.Ql ${VAR-word} ,
.Ql ${VAR:+word}
and
.Ql ${VAR+word} ,
with variables in
.Ar word
expanded too.
In double quotes a backslash escapes only
.Ql \(dq ,
.Ql $
and itself, and is kept before anything else.
.Cm ENV
and
.Cm LABEL
names are expanded as their values are.
.It
.Cm SHELL ,
kept in the image for the builds that use it as a base, and used for
the shell forms of
.Cm RUN , CMD
and
.Cm ENTRYPOINT .
.Cm ENTRYPOINT
resets a
.Cm CMD
the base image gave, not one given before it in the same stage.
.It
.Cm WORKDIR ,
relative to the one before, with
.Ql ..
going up (never above
.Pa / ) .
Directories it makes belong to the
.Cm USER
in force.
.It
.Cm FROM Fl -platform
and the
.Ev BUILDPLATFORM , TARGETPLATFORM , TARGETOS
and
.Ev TARGETARCH
arguments.
jailer builds only for the host's platform, and refuses any other.
.El
.Pp
.Fl -strict
fails the build when the compatibility check reports an error.
.Fl -target
stops at the named stage, which is the one checked.
.Fl -squash
flattens the image, its base included, into one layer.
Layers keep file flags
.Pf ( Cm schg ,
.Cm uchg ,
.Cm nodump
and the other append-only and no-unlink flags, but not
.Cm uarch ,
which ZFS sets on every write) and hard links; a layer holds every name
of a hard-linked file it changes.
Named pipes are kept; sockets and devices are not.
A file whose name begins with
.Ql .wh.
fails the build: in a layer that name removes a file.
.Pp
.Fl -cache-to
pushes this build's
.Cm RUN
steps to
.Ar ref
in a registry, as an OCI manifest of their layers;
.Fl -cache-from ,
which may be given more than once, lets a build on any host take a step
from such a cache rather than run it, downloading only the layers of the
steps it takes.
A cache that is missing is skipped with a warning.
.Pp
New layers are gzip-compressed, or with
.Fl -compression Cm zstd
zstd-compressed, which pulls and unpacks faster; Docker 23 and later,
Podman and Legion read both.
A cached layer is reused only by builds compressing as it was.
With a ZFS store, each stage is a clone of its base image or of the
stage it is built on, rather than a copy.
.Pp
.Fl -label
sets a label on the image, over the Containerfile's
.Cm LABEL ;
.Fl -annotation
an annotation on its manifest, which
.Nm push
and
.Nm export
keep.
Files in layers carry a fixed time, and the image config its base
image's time, so the same build gives the same image;
.Fl -timestamp
(or
.Ev SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH )
dates the config and this build's history, in seconds since 1970.
.Pp
.Cm RUN
steps run in a jail with
.Ql allow.chflags ,
no securelevel and devfs ruleset 4.
.Fl -param
sets another
.Xr jail 8
parameter for them, e.g.
.Ql --param allow.raw_sockets ;
.Fl -sysv
gives them SysV IPC of their own
.Pf ( Ql sysvshm=new ) .
.Fl m ,
.Fl -cpus ,
.Fl -cpuset-cpus
and
.Fl -pids-limit
limit each step as
.Nm run
does a container.
.Fl -backend Cm buildah
delegates the build to
.Xr buildah 1
and imports the result; it refuses
.Fl -param ,
.Fl -sysv ,
.Fl -cache-from ,
.Fl -cache-to
and the limits above, which only the native builder has.
.It Xo
.Nm run
.Op Fl dit
.Op Fl -allow-mlock
.Op Fl -linux
.Op Fl -rm
.Op Fl -vnet
.Op Fl e Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl u Ar user Ns Op : Ns Ar group
.Op Fl v Ar src : Ns Ar dst Ns Op :ro
.Op Fl w Ar dir
.Op Fl -entrypoint Ar path
.Op Fl -hostname Ar name
.Op Fl m Ar size
.Op Fl -cpus Ar n
.Op Fl -cpuset-cpus Ar list
.Op Fl -name Ar name
.Op Fl -network Cm host | bridge | none
.Op Fl p Oo Ar ip : Oc Ns Ar port : Ns Ar port Ns Op / Ns Cm tcp | udp
.Op Fl -param Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl -pids-limit Ar n
.Op Fl -sysv Cm msg , Ns Cm sem , Ns Cm shm
.Op Ar image Op Ar command ...
.Xc
Run
.Ar image
with Legion's own jail runtime, as a node runs a container: a jail of
its own on the host's network, devfs ruleset 4, and the image's user
resolved against its own
.Pa /etc/passwd
and
.Pa /etc/group .
It has Legion's security defaults:
.Ql enforce_statfs=2 ,
.Ql securelevel=2 ,
.Ql allow.raw_sockets=false
and
.Ql allow.chflags=false .
Without
.Ar image ,
the current directory's built image is run; an image missing from the
local store is pulled.
Needs root.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Run in the background and print the container ID.
When a container with a pod
.Pq any network but Cm host
exits, a process of its own stops the pod, freeing its address and
ports; the container is kept, stopped, as
.Cm stop
leaves it.
.It Fl i , Fl t
Keep standard input open; allocate a pseudo-terminal.
.It Fl e
Set an environment variable.
.It Fl u
Run as
.Ar user ,
a name or UID, optionally with a group.
.It Fl v
Mount host path
.Ar src
at
.Ar dst
with
.Xr nullfs 4 ,
read-only with
.Ql :ro .
.It Fl w
Working directory inside the container.
.It Fl -rm
Remove the container when it exits.
With
.Fl d ,
a process of its own waits for that and removes it.
.It Fl -vnet
Run it as a node runs a pod: a VNET jail running nothing, named
.Ar name Ns Li -pod ,
with
.Li lo0
up on 127.0.0.1, and the container a jail inside it.
The pod gets an
.Xr epair 4
on jailer's bridge,
.Li jailer0
(10.77.0.1/16), an address from 10.77.0.0/16 and a default route through
the bridge; pf translates its traffic out of the host's default
interface.
.Nm
refuses to start a pod while another interface, such as a VPN's, routes
those addresses.
.Nm stop
and
.Nm rm
take the pod along, and free its address.
Same as
.Fl -network Cm bridge .
.It Fl -network Cm host | bridge | none
.Cm host
(the default): the host's network, in a jail of its own.
.Cm bridge :
as
.Fl -vnet .
.Cm none :
a pod with only its loopback.
.It Fl p , Fl -publish Oo Ar ip : Oc Ns Ar host : Ns Ar container Ns Op / Ns Cm tcp | udp
Publish the container's port
.Ar container
on the host's port
.Ar host ,
on every address of the host or only
.Ar ip
.Pf ( Li 0.0.0.0
is every address of the host);
traffic a routing host passes on to other machines is left alone.
It implies
.Fl -vnet .
Other machines reach it; the host itself reaches the container at its
own address, which
.Nm ps
shows.
.It Fl -allow-mlock
Allow
.Xr mlock 2 .
.It Fl -sysv
Give the jail the host's SysV IPC of the listed kinds, as Legion's
.Ql sysvipc
does.
.It Fl -param
Set a
.Xr jail 8
parameter, over the security defaults, e.g.
.Ql --param securelevel=-1 .
.It Fl m , Fl -memory Ar size
Kill a process of the container whose resident memory goes past
.Ar size
(e.g.
.Ql 512m ) ,
as a Legion node does.
.It Fl -cpus Ar n
Hold the container to
.Ar n
CPUs' time (e.g.
.Ql 1.5 ) .
.It Fl -pids-limit Ar n
Allow the container at most
.Ar n
processes.
.It Fl -cpuset-cpus Ar list
Run the container on the CPUs in
.Ar list ,
as
.Xr cpuset 1
lists them (e.g.
.Ql 0-3,6 ) ,
set on its jail before anything runs in it.
.El
.Pp
The limits are
.Xr rctl 8
rules on the container's jail, which need
.Ql kern.racct.enable=1
in
.Xr loader.conf 5 ;
.Nm rm
drops them.
.Pp
.Fl -vnet
and
.Fl p
need
.Xr pf 4 ,
with
.Xr pf.conf 5
calling jailer's anchors, the first two before any filter rule:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nat-anchor "jailer/*"
rdr-anchor "jailer/*"
anchor "jailer/*"
.Ed
.Pp
Each address taken is a file in
.Pa /var/run/jailer/net ,
so containers of different state directories never share one.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -linux
Run a Linux image through
.Xr linux 4 ,
mounting linprocfs, linsysfs, fdescfs and
.Pa /dev/shm .
.El
.It Xo
.Nm push
.Op Fl -authfile Ar path
.Op Fl -creds Ar user Ns Op : Ns Ar password
.Op Fl -sign-key Ar path
.Op Ar source
.Ar target
.Xc
Push
.Ar source ,
by default the current project's image, to the registry reference
.Ar target .
.Fl -sign-key
then signs it, as
.Nm sign
does.
.It Xo
.Nm sign
.Fl -key Ar path
.Op Fl -authfile Ar path
.Op Fl -creds Ar user Ns Op : Ns Ar password
.Ar reference
.Xc
Sign the manifest or index a registry holds for
.Ar reference
with the private key at
.Ar path ,
as
.Xr cosign 1
does with a key pair: a simple-signing payload naming the digest, signed
with ECDSA P-256 and pushed under the tag
.Li sha256- Ns Ar hex Ns Li .sig
in the same repository, beside any signatures already there.
No transparency log entry is made, so
.Ql cosign verify --key
needs
.Fl -insecure-ignore-tlog .
.It Xo
.Nm verify
.Fl -key Ar path
.Op Fl -authfile Ar path
.Op Fl -creds Ar user Ns Op : Ns Ar password
.Ar reference
.Xc
Check that the image
.Ar reference
names carries a signature by the public key at
.Ar path ,
and exit non-zero if it does not.
.It Nm key generate Op Ar prefix
Write a new P-256 key pair:
.Ar prefix Ns Pa .key ,
the private key (PKCS#8 PEM, mode 0600), and
.Ar prefix Ns Pa .pub ,
the public key, by default
.Pa jailer.key
and
.Pa jailer.pub .
.Ql cosign import-key-pair
turns them into cosign's own key format.
.It Xo
.Nm export
.Op Fl o Ar path
.Op Fl -name Ar ref
.Op Ar image
.Op Ar output.tar
.Xc
Write an OCI archive suitable for
.Ql legion image load .
It also holds Docker's
.Pa manifest.json ,
for
.Ql podman load
and
.Ql docker load .
.Fl -name
overrides the reference recorded in the archive.
.It Nm check Op Ar target
Check a directory's
.Pa Containerfile ,
a local image reference, or by default the current directory's
.Pa Containerfile
and built image against Legion's runtime:
the image user, an executable entrypoint, the stop signal and the
platform.
.It Xo
.Nm pull
.Op Fl -linux
.Op Fl -authfile Ar path
.Op Fl -creds Ar user Ns Op : Ns Ar password
.Ar reference
.Xc
Pull an image into the local store.
.Fl -linux
accepts a Linux image when the registry offers no FreeBSD one.
.It Xo
.Nm manifest push
.Op Fl -authfile Ar path
.Op Fl -creds Ar user Ns Op : Ns Ar password
.Op Fl -sign-key Ar path
.Ar target
.Ar source ...
.Xc
Push an image index to
.Ar target
naming one image per platform, so that nodes of each architecture pull
their own.
Each
.Ar source
is a local image, pushed by digest into
.Ar target Ns 's
repository, or an image or index already in that repository, such as
one pushed from a build host of another architecture.
Two sources for the same platform are refused.
.It Xo
.Nm manifest inspect
.Op Fl -authfile Ar path
.Op Fl -creds Ar user Ns Op : Ns Ar password
.Ar reference
.Xc
Print the manifest or index a registry holds for
.Ar reference ,
and its digest on standard error.
.It Nm load Oo Fl -name Ar ref Oc Ar archive.tar
Load an OCI archive into the local store.
.It Xo
.Nm login
.Op Fl -authfile Ar path
.Fl u Ar username
.Fl p Ar password | Fl -password-stdin
.Ar registry
.Xc
Check credentials with
.Ar registry ,
as
.Ql docker login
does, and save them to
.Pa ~/.docker/config.json
or
.Ar path ;
credentials the registry refuses are not saved.
Only the registry's entry changes: the file's other settings, such as
.Ql credsStore ,
are kept, and a file that is not valid JSON is left as it is.
With
.Fl -password-stdin
the password or token is the first line of standard input, kept out of
the command line, where other users can see it:
.Dl gh auth token | jailer login -u me --password-stdin ghcr.io
.It Nm images
List local images.
.It Nm rmi Ar reference
Remove a local image, along with any layers and blobs no other image or
build cache entry uses.
.It Nm prune Op Fl -cache
Remove build cache entries whose layer is gone, then the layers and blobs
nothing uses.
With
.Fl -cache ,
clear the build cache first, freeing the layers only it kept, and the
directories of
.Cm RUN Fl -mount Ns = Ns Cm type=cache
that no build is using.
.It Nm ps Op Fl a
List running containers, or all with
.Fl a .
.It Nm logs Oo Fl n Ar lines Oc Ar container
Print a container's output, or its last
.Ar lines .
.It Nm exec Oo Fl it Oc Ar container Ar command ...
Run a command in a running container.
A signal ending
.Nm
ends the command too.
With
.Fl i
or
.Fl t ,
SIGINT and SIGQUIT are left to the command, which gets them from the terminal.
Needs root.
.It Nm stop Oo Fl t Ar seconds Oc Ar container
Stop a container, killing it after
.Ar seconds .
.It Nm rm Oo Fl f Oc Ar container ...
Remove containers.
A running container is refused unless
.Fl f
is given, which stops it first.
.El
.Pp
A signal ending
.Nm build
stops the running step and takes its jail, mounts and limits with it.
.Nm run
leaves SIGINT and SIGQUIT to the command, in its process group, and cleans up
once the command ends; SIGTERM or SIGHUP ends the command too.
Before the command has started, any of them removes the container once it is
made, and nothing runs.
